- Replication ke do common approaches samajhte hain:

1. SYNCHRONOUS REPLICATION
---------------------------

- Primary DB write complete hone ke baad Replica(s) se required acknowledgements ka wait karta hai — ye ACK Replica(s) par change successfully apply hone ke baad milte hain. 
  Phir Primary DB Application ko success bhejta hai, aur Application Client ko. Zaroori nahi ki Primary DB har Replica ka ACK wait kare — kitne ACK chahiye aur kaunse Replicas
  se, ye replication configuration par depend karta hai.

- ### BENEFIT :
    → Primary DB fail hone par bhi, jinke ACK receive ho chuke hain un Replica(s) ke paas latest acknowledged write available rehti hai isliye strong durability guarantee milti hai.


- ### LIMITATION :

   a) HIGHER WRITE LATENCY 
        → Primary DB Replica(s) ke ACK ka wait karta hai — client ko success response late milta hai, aur write latency badh jaati hai.

   b) PRIMARY DB KI REPLICA(S) PAR TIGHT COUPLING 

        → Primary DB ko client ko write success dene se pehle configured policy ke according Replica(s) se required ACKs ka wait karna padta hai isliye write 
          acknowledgement Replica(s) ki availability aur performance par depend karta hai.

        → Agar Replica slow ho, to ACK late aata hai, jiski wajah se write acknowledgement Application aur Client tak delay ho jaata hai.

        → Agar Replica unavailable ho, to required ACKs nahi milte — Primary DB configured timeout tak wait karta hai. Timeout ke baad do possibilities hain
           1) Ya to write Primary DB par committed reh sakti hai but client ko failure mile - client ko lagega write fail hui, jabki data actually persist ho chuka hai 
              isliye blindly retry karne se duplicate write ka risk hota hai, idempotency zaroori hai. 

           2) Ya write rollback ho jaye aur client ko failure mile.



2. ASYNCHRONOUS REPLICATION 
-----------------------------

- Primary DB write complete karne ke baad Replica(s) ke ACK ka wait nahi karta — application ko jaldi success deta hai, jisse client ko jaldi response milta hai. 
  Primary DB ke changes uske baad asynchronously Replica(s) tak propagate/apply hote rehte hain.

- Replication instant nahi hoti — Primary DB par change hone aur Replica par apply hone ke beech jo delay hota hai, use REPLICATION LAG kehte hain.

  Example : User ne profile name Anand → Anurag update kiya, aur application ne Primary DB update ke baad Redis aur Local Cache invalidate kar diya. 
            Primary DB par name Anurag ho gaya, but Replica par latest change abhi apply nahi hua tha — abhi bhi Anand. Kyunki Redis aur Local Cache 
            me old value nahi thi, next read Replica se serve hui — aur user ko stale name "Anand" mila, jabki Primary DB me already "Anurag" hai. 
            Is situation ko Stale Read kehte hain.

- Replication ka apna overhead hota hai: Primary DB ko changes log karne padte hain (jaise MySQL me Binlog), changes network ke through Replicas tak jaate hain (network overhead), 
  Replicas ko unhe apply karna padta hai — jiske liye additional storage lagta hai Replica DB me, aur eventually replication lag bhi aata hai, jisse stale data serve hota hai.

- Read Replicas me asynchronous replication commonly use hoti hai — kyunki Primary DB ko Replica(s) ke ACK ka wait nahi karna padta, isliye client ko 
  write success jaldi milta hai aur client-facing write latency kam rehti hai. Note: Write execution fast nahi hui — sirf end-to-end acknowledgement 
  fast hua. Trade-off: Replication complete hone tak Replica(s) Primary DB se peeche reh sakti hain — us duration me Replica se aane wali reads me stale 
  data mil sakta hai.


- ### BENEFIT :

    a) LOWER WRITE LATENCY
       → Primary DB ko Replica(s) ke ACK ka wait nahi karna padta — isliye application ko jaldi success milta hai aur client ko bhi jaldi response milta hai, jisse better user experience milta hai.

    b) LOOSE COUPLING WITH REPLICA(s)
       → Synchronous ke unlike, Asynchronous Replication me Primary DB ko Replica(s) ke ACK ka wait nahi karna padta — isliye koi Replica temporarily slow ya unavailable ho, to Primary DB application 
         ko (aur uske through client ko) write success acknowledgement dene ke liye un Replicas par depend nahi karta.


- ### LIMITATION :

    a) REPLICATION LAG
        → Primary DB par change hone aur Replica par apply hone ke beech ka time delay.

    b) RECENT WRITE LOST ON PRIMARY FAILURE
        → Primary DB par write complete hui, but change abhi Replica(s) par apply nahi hua tha. Primary ne Application (aur hence client) ko success de diya, aur uske turant baad Primary fail ho gaya.
          Ab agar isi Replica ko new Primary promote kiya jaye, to recently acknowledged write us Replica par available nahi hogi — system ke perspective se wo write lost ho jaati hai.



------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: KYA HAR READ REQUEST REPLICA SE AAYEGI?
------------------------------------------------------

Nahi. Application decide karti hai — stale data acceptable hai ya latest chahiye.

- Stale acceptable 
     → Replica se serve karo — isse read workload multiple replicas me distribute ho jaata hai.
     → Example: Product Listing, Product Details, Blog Posts, Public Feed.

- Latest required 
     → Primary DB se serve karo - kyunki replication lag hota hai us duran Replica ke paas purana data ho sakta hai
     → Example: Account Balance, Payment Status, Critical Transaction Status.


--------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: REPLICATION LAG KAISE HANDLE KARE ?
--------------------------------------------------
Common strategies:

 1. Read-your-write: User ne abhi update kiya to immediate next read Primary DB se karo — baad wali reads Replica(s) se.

 2. Critical reads Primary se: Bank balance, payment status, order confirmation — always Primary DB se. 
                              Slight staleness acceptable ho to Replica se — jaise product listing, blog posts, catalog, public feed.



-----------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: READ AND WRITE REQUEST KA FLOW KYA HOTA HAI?
-----------------------------------------------------------

### READ FLOW:
--------------

Client request → DNS → CDN (static + hit → return) → API Gateway → Service LB → App Instance → Local Cache (hit → return) → Redis (hit → populate Local Cache → return) → Read Replica (fetch → populate Redis + Local Cache → return).

### WRITE FLOW:
---------------

Client request → DNS → CDN → API Gateway → Service LB → App Instance → Primary DB write (fail → error return; success → invalidate/update Local Cache across all instances + relevant Redis node(s)) → return success.



-----------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: READ AUR WRITE REQUESTS KA ROUTING KAISE HOTA HAI ?
-----------------------------------------------------------------

a) Application-side:

   - Read case:   App Instance ke paas Replicas ki list hoti hai (config ya service discovery se), aur wo khud load balancing algorithm se ek Replica select karti hai 
                  jaise Round Robin, Least Connections, Weighted. Generally same-region wali Replica prefer karte hain — network latency kam.

   - Write case:  App Instance Write requests ko directly Primary DB par route karta hain.


b) DB-level LB: App Instance ke paas sirf DB LB ka endpoint hota hai (jaise ProxySQL, HAProxy, AWS RDS Proxy).

  a) L4 (TCP) LB:  Generally L4 use karte hain — is case me App Instance ne request ko read/write me classify kar liya hota hai.
                   Write hai to App Instance directly Primary DB par likhti hai; 
                   Read hai to App Instance L4 LB ko read request route kar deti hai, aur L4 LB read requests ko available Replicas me distribute karta hai.

  b) L7 DB Proxy: Jaise ProxySQL — SQL parse karke SELECT ko Replica pe, INSERT/UPDATE ko Primary pe route karta hai. App Instance ko kuch change nahi karna padta.



--------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: PRIMARY DB FAIL HO JAAYE TO KYA HOTA HAI?
--------------------------------------------------------

Application naye writes perform nahi kar paayegi. High Availability setup me system ek healthy Read Replica ko new Primary DB ke taur par promote kar deta hai
ise Failover kehte hain. Uske baad writes new Primary DB par jaate hain, aur baaki Replicas new Primary se replication continue karte hain. Isliye replication
sirf read scaling nahi High Availability bhi deta hai.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: SYSTEM KO KAISE PATA CHALEGA KI PRIMARY DB FAIL HO GAYA HAI?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

- Monitoring/Failover system periodically Primary DB ko check karte hain — "reachable hai aur requests handle kar raha hai?" 
  Agar Multiple consecutive health checks fail ho to system suspected failed maan leta hai.

- But ek important problem hai - response na aana hamesha fail hone ka matlab nahi. Ho sakta hai Primary healthy ho, sirf network issue se unreachable ho
  Agar system is situation me turant failover kar de, to SPLIT BRAIN ho sakta hai — do Primary ek saath active, dono writes accept kar rahe hain
  Isliye single failed check par failover nahi hota — multiple failed checks + configured timeout ke baad hi hota hai.


------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: SPLIT BRAIN DANGEROUS KYUN HAI?
------------------------------------------------

Data inconsistency aur conflicting writes ka risk — dono Primary same data ke liye alag-alag history maintain karne lagte hain.

Example :

- Initially cluster me Primary DB aur 3 Read Replicas hain — sab par `name = 'Rahul'` same hai.

- Network issue ki wajah se monitoring/failover system ne galti se Primary ko failed maan liya aur Replica 1 ko new Primary promote kar diya.

- Ab do Primary active hain — do different update requests do different Primaries par gayi:
     → Pehli Original Primary par write hui `name = 'Anurag'`, 
     → Dusri naye Primary (Replica 1) par write hui `name = 'Anand'`. 

  Dono apni-apni history maintain kar rahe hain.

- Ideal case: Agar dono writes ek hi Primary par sequentially hoti, to history hoti — `Rahul → Anurag → Anand`.

- Actual case (Split Brain):
         → Original Primary ki history — `Rahul → Anurag`.
         → Naye Primary ki history — `Rahul → Anand`.

- Network restore hone par dono ki state different hai — system ko decide karna padta hai kaunsi state authoritative hai. 
    → Agar Original Primary authoritative maana jaye, to naye Primary ki `Rahul → Anand` write discard ho jaati hai —
    → Aur agar naya Primary (Replica 1) authoritative maana jaye, to Original Primary ki `Rahul → Anurag` write discard ho jaati hai.

- Split Brain ke baad dono Primaries ko simply synchronize nahi kiya ja sakta — system ko conflicting history me se ek state authoritative 
  choose karni padti hai, aur doosri write discard ho jaati hai.


----------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: SPLIT BRAIN KO PREVENT KAISE KAREIN?
----------------------------------------------------

Split Brain prevent karne ka main goal ye hai ki database cluster me ek hi time par sirf 
ek hi Primary node ho — jisse sirf wahi writes accept kare.

Iske liye Monitoring / Failover mechanisms different techniques use karte hain.

1: FENCING - Failover ke time original Primary ko network se isolate karo — writes accept na kar sake

2. QUORUM / CONSENSUS BASED DECISION - Failover ka decision kisi ek single monitoring node ke observation ke basis par nahi liya jaata. 
                                       Multiple monitoring/coordination nodes ki agreement ke baad hi Replica ko New Primary promote kiya 
                                       jaata hai.


---------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: MANUAL VS AUTOMATIC FAILOVER ?
---------------------------------------------

Failover do tarah se ho sakta hai:

1. AUTOMATIC FAILOVER
-----------------------

Monitoring / Failover System Primary DB failure detect karke
automatically kisi healthy Replica ko New Primary DB ke role mein
promote kar deta hai.


2. MANUAL FAILOVER
---------------------

- Human / DBA situation verify karke manually kisi healthy Replica ko
  New Primary ke role mein promote karta hai.

- Ye approach tab use hoti hai jab automatic failover configured nahi hota ya failover 
  ka decision lene se pehle human verification aur approval zaroori hota hai.





