#11. CAP THEOREM 
===================

-------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: NETWORK PARTITION KYA HAI ?
-------------------------------------------

Network partition = System Architecture ke kisi bhi do components ke beech communication toot jaana due to network issue, chahe wo components alive hon.

- Application ↔ Database / Primary Database / Primary Shard
- Primary Database / Shard ↔ Read Replicas
- Service ↔ Service (REST API, gRPC, Async communication etc.)
- Application ↔ Shard (In cross-shard query)
- Txn Coordinator ↔ Shard (In cross-shard transaction — 2PC)


------------------------------------
## QUESTION: CAP THEOREM KYA HAI ?
------------------------------------

CAP theorem kehta hai distributed system mein Consistency, Availability, Partition Tolerance — teeno nahi mil sakte.
Distributed systems mein network partition ho sakta hai. Network partition ek "choice" nahi hai — wo real world mein hamesha ho sakta hai.
Ab choice CP ya AP mein se karni padti hai.


### QUESTION: CP KYA HAI ?
----------------------------

- CP = Consistency + Partition Tolerance.
- Matlab: Network partition ho jaye toh system available rehne ke bajay consistency choose karta hai — 
  yaani galat/stale data dene se better hai ki request fail kar do (error de do).
- Example — Critical Account Balance Read

Maan lo user ne ₹10,000 ka payment kiya aur ye write request Primary DB par gayi. Primary DB mein account balance successfully update ho gaya.

Uske baad Primary DB aur Read Replica ke beech network partition ho gaya, isliye latest balance Replica par replicate nahi ho paya.

Primary DB          X X X          Read Replica
Latest Balance                     Old Balance


## IMPORTANT
---------------

Typical Primary-Replica setup mein, Primary DB regularly Replica ko heartbeat bhejta hai.
Agar Replica ko configured timeout tak heartbeat nahi milta, toh monitoring/failover system detect kar sakta hai 
ki Primary aur Replica ke beech network communication fail ho gaya hai.

Ab agar next read request Replica par jaati hai aur System ko detect ho chuka hai ki Primary aur Replica ke beech 
communication nahi ho raha, to.

CP approach:

"Mere paas latest account balance nahi hai, isliye main potentially stale balance return nahi karunga. Request fail karwa dunga."

➡️ Consistency maintained
➡️ Availability sacrificed


## NOTE 
---------
Real systems mein critical reads, jaise account balance/payment status, often Primary DB se read kiye ja sakte hain.
Replica-read example ka purpose sirf CAP ke partition ke time Consistency vs Availability trade-off ko samjhana hai.



### QUESTION: AP KYA HAI ?
----------------------------

- AP = Availability + Partition Tolerance.
- Matlab: Network partition ho jaye toh system consistency chhod ke available rehta hai — 
  yaani har request ka jawab deta hai, chahe data thoda stale ho.
- Example — Product Catalog Read
  
  Maan lo product stock update ki write request Primary DB par gai and Primary DB mein product stock successfully update ho gaya.

  Uske baad Primary DB aur Read Replica ke beech network partition ho gaya, isliye latest stock Replica par replicate nahi ho paya.

  Primary DB          X X X          Read Replica
  Latest stock                       Old Stock

  Ab agar next read/view request Replica par jaati hai aur System ko detect ho chuka hai ki Primary aur Replica ke beech 
  communication nahi ho raha, toh.

  AP approach:

  "Primary se communication nahi ho raha, lekin user ko response dena hai. Mere paas jo data available hai, main wahi return karunga, chahe woh thoda stale ho."

Replica 10 units return kar dega, even though Primary par actual latest value 5 units ho chuki ho.

➡️ Availability maintained
➡️ Consistency temporarily sacrificed



### QUESTION: SINGLE-NODE SYSTEM MEIN CA KYUN POSSIBLE HAI?
-------------------------------------------------------------

Single-node system mein sirf ek hi node hota hai — koi doosra node nahi, koi network communication nahi.

- P (Partition Tolerance): Doosra node hi nahi hai, toh partition ka scenario hi nahi banta → P ki zaroorat nahi.
- C (Consistency): Sirf ek node hai, isliye multiple nodes ke beech data inconsistency ka issue nahi hota.
- A (Availability): Jab tak node up hai, wo har request ko respond kar sakta hai.

➡️ Isliye single-node system mein C + A = CA possible hai.



======================================
#12. CONSISTENCY AND AVAILABILITY
======================================

-------------------------------------------
### QUESTION: STRONG CONSISTENCY KYA HAI ? 
-------------------------------------------

- Har read latest write dikhata hai. Koi stale data nahi.

- Example: Replication Consistency
  Agar replication ko synchronously perform kiya jaye, toh strong consistency achieve ki ja sakti hai.

- Example: Distributed Transaction Consistency 
  2PC (Two-Phase Commit) strong consistency provide karta hai — kyunki wo multiple shards / databases
  ke beech distributed transaction ko all-or-nothing manner mein coordinate karta hai.


---------------------------------------------
### QUESTION: EVENTUAL CONSISTENCY KYA HAI ? 
---------------------------------------------

- Write ke turant baad read stale data de sakta hai, lekin eventually saare nodes sync ho jaate hain — 
  yaani eventually sab jagah latest data reflect ho jaata hai.

- Example: Replication Consistency
  Agar replication ko asynchronously perform kiya jaye, toh eventual consistency achieve ki ja sakti hai.

- Example: Distributed Transaction Consistency
  SAGA pattern eventual consistency provide karta hai — kyunki wo failed local transaction ko rollback karne 
  ke liye compensation transaction chalata hai, jisse thodi der tak involved databases inconsistent state mein
  aa sakte hain. Lekin eventually compensation transactions ke successful execution ke baad sabhi involved 
  databases eventually consistent state mein aa jaate hain.


---------------------------------------
### QUESTION: AVAILABILITY KYA HAI ? 
---------------------------------------

- Availability ek guarantee hai ki system jitne time available hai, utne time mein wo incoming
  request ka response de — chahe data stale ho, chahe kuch nodes/components down hon, request fail
  nahi honi chahiye.
- Highly Available System Examples: Social Media Feed, CDN, DNS.



